WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.293
A consumer is a type that only takes values of T in.

2
00:00:06.293 --> 00:00:09.374
An audit log, an event handler, a Comparator.

3
00:00:09.374 --> 00:00:12.865
You hand it a T, and it hands nothing typed T back.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.984
Here is forwardAuthorizations.

5
00:00:14.984 --> 00:00:19.349
It holds card authorizations, and it needs a sink to pass them to.

6
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:21.139
And here is an audit log.

7
00:00:21.139 --> 00:00:23.451
It accepts any payment event at all.

8
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.383
Can the audit log stand in?

9
00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:28.883
Run the substitution test again, the other way round.

10
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:31.484
Every call passes a CardAuthorized.

11
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:35.513
The audit log takes every PaymentEvent, so it takes these too.

12
00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:40.132
So for a consumer, the more general type is the one that can stand in.

13
00:00:40.132 --> 00:00:44.725
A Sink of PaymentEvent works where a Sink of CardAuthorized is expected.

14
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:47.749
The other way fails, and it should.

15
00:00:47.749 --> 00:00:53.160
A Sink of CardAuthorized might be a fraud check built only for card authorizations.

16
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:56.504
Suppose you could view it as a Sink of PaymentEvent.

17
00:00:56.504 --> 00:00:58.637
Then anyone could hand it a refund.

18
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.132
So the compiler refuses that view.

19
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:03.662
Here is the whole picture.

20
00:01:03.662 --> 00:01:06.701
CardAuthorized is a subtype of PaymentEvent.

21
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:08.655
A producer keeps the arrow.

22
00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:12.299
A Source of CardAuthorized is a Source of PaymentEvent.

23
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:17.103
Start the consumer the same way, with Sink of CardAuthorized on the bottom.

24
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:20.797
But the Sink of PaymentEvent is the one that stands in.

25
00:01:20.797 --> 00:01:22.466
So it swaps to the bottom.

26
00:01:22.466 --> 00:01:24.007
The subtype arrow flips.

27
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:25.892
This is contravariance.

28
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:30.572
Kotlin declares this once, on the interface, with in.

29
00:01:30.572 --> 00:01:32.931
Java writes ? super at each use.

30
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:37.319
Either way, forwardAuthorizations now accepts the audit log.

31
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.568
Java programmers learn this as PECS.

32
00:01:40.568 --> 00:01:42.860
Producer extends, consumer super.

33
00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.639
It is the same rule, with Java spelling.

34
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.338
So for each parameter, ask one question.

35
00:01:48.338 --> 00:01:51.637
Does it give you values of T, or take them from you?

36
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.533
Give: out, or ? extends.

37
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:56.924
Take: in, or ? super.

38
00:01:56.924 --> 00:01:58.519
Both: plain T.
